, -0.00904595572501421, 0.03400978073477745, 0.02180500514805317, -0.0036243018694221973, 0.048044197261333466, 0.017090991139411926, -0.024043623358011246, 0.0012625862145796418, -0.01912512071430683, -0.03157743811607361, 0.05971084535121918, 0.028671536594629288, -0.010724919848144054, 0.007517668418586254, 0.02040586993098259, 0.0018699999200180173, -0.02688494697213173, 0.01574566774070263, 0.005160661414265633, -0.0011758129112422466, 0.012452315539121628, 0.010832546278834343, -0.0038691507652401924, 0.0217727180570364, -0.01239850278943777, 0.04778589680790901, 0.0058709923177957535, 0.015401264652609825, 0.006565179210156202, -0.03579637035727501, -0.039757005870342255, -0.01797352358698845, 0.023311767727136612, 0.031233033165335655, 0.045375075191259384]], "input": ["VITAMINA D:VITAMINA D, also known as Vitamin D, is an essential fat-soluble nutrient crucial for maintaining bone health and regulating calcium and phosphate levels in the body. It plays a significant role in calcium absorption, which is vital for preventing conditions such as osteoporosis, rickets, and osteomalacia. Deficiencies in Vitamin D are common, particularly among older adults, individuals with limited sun exposure, and those with certain health conditions, including chronic kidney disease, cirrhosis, and inflammatory bowel disease. \n\nThe recommended daily intake of Vitamin D varies, with suggestions typically ranging from 400 to 1000 IU, depending on age and health status. For instance, infants, especially those who are exclusively breastfed, are advised to receive a daily supplement of 400 IU to prevent deficiency. In older adults, adequate levels of Vitamin D are essential for maintaining muscle strength and reducing the risk of falls and fractures.\n\nVitamin D can be synthesized in the skin upon exposure to sunlight, but it is also found in dietary sources such as fatty fish, eggs, and fortified dairy products. In certain populations, such as those with obesity or undergoing bariatric surgery, supplementation may be necessary to achieve optimal levels. Additionally, Vitamin D is often monitored in patients receiving parenteral nutrition and those with elevated parathyroid hormone (PTH) levels.\n\nBeyond its role in bone health, Vitamin D has been associated with various other health benefits, including potential protective effects against certain cancers, cardiovascular diseases, and cognitive decline. It is also important for immune function and may influence insulin secretion and glucose metabolism.\n\nIn summary, VITAMINA D is a vital nutrient that supports overall health, particularly bone and muscle function, and is often supplemented in various populations to prevent deficiencies and promote well-being.", "VITAMINA E:VITAMINA E, también conocida como vitamina E, es un antioxidante liposoluble que desempeña un papel crucial en la protección de las células del daño oxidativo. Se encuentra en diversos alimentos, como nueces, aceites vegetales, verduras de hoja verde y huevos. Este nutriente es importante no solo para la salud celular, sino también para la salud ósea y la regulación de los niveles de magnesio. \n\nLa vitamina E actúa protegiendo las membranas lipídicas y ha sido estudiada por su potencial en la salud cerebral, incluyendo su relación con la cognición y la demencia. Además, se ha observado que puede ayudar a reducir el estrés oxidativo en pacientes con enfermedades inflamatorias intestinales durante episodios moderados. \n\nSin embargo, se debe tener precaución con su uso en dosis altas, especialmente en pacientes que reciben quimioterapia o radioterapia, ya que no se recomienda en estas circunstancias. Las recomendaciones diarias sugieren una administración de 10 U.I. de vitamina E. También se incluye en la nutrición parenteral y en las pautas de nutrición infantil, destacando su importancia en diversas etapas de la vida y en diferentes condiciones de salud. \n\nEn resumen, la vitamina E es un antioxidante esencial que protege las células del daño, contribuye a la salud ósea y cerebral, y debe ser administrada con cuidado en ciertas condiciones médicas.", "VITAMINA K:VITAMINA K, also known as Vitamin K, is an essential fat-soluble nutrient crucial for various bodily functions, particularly blood clotting and bone metabolism. It is produced by intestinal bacteria and is found in various foods, especially green leafy vegetables and some animal products. This vitamin plays a key role in the activation of several blood coagulation factors, making it vital for preventing hemorrhagic diseases, particularly in newborns, for whom it is recommended to be administered intramuscularly at birth to prevent transient deficiencies in coagulation factors.\n\nIn patients with celiac disease, Vitamin K deficiency can occur, leading to increased risk of hemorrhagic conditions. Additionally, certain medications can affect Vitamin K levels, resulting in increased requirements for this nutrient. It is important to note that there are no established upper limits for Vitamin K due to a lack of data on adverse effects. However, it can antagonize the therapeutic action of oral anticoagulants when present in enteral nutrition.\n\nVitamin K is also recommended for patients with coagulation issues to improve blood clotting before medical procedures. In cases of cholestasis, specific recommendations for supplementation are made to ensure adequate levels. Regular administration of Vitamin K is advised, typically 1-2 times a week, as it is not included in most commercial preparations. Overall, Vitamin K is a critical nutrient for maintaining health, particularly in relation to blood coagulation and bone health.", "VITAMINA B1:VITAMINA B1, also known as Thiamine, is an essential water-soluble nutrient that plays a crucial role in energy metabolism. It is particularly important for converting carbohydrates into energy, making it a vital component of nutritional recommendations for various populations, including patients on nutritional support and infants. The recommended daily intake of Vitamin B1 is 3 mg, and it is available in various forms, such as Benerva 100 mg/ml and Benerva 300 mg. Due to its significance in maintaining health, Vitamin B1 is advised for administration to enhance overall well-being."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "temperature": 0.0, "frequency_penalty": null, "presence_penalty": null, "top_p": 1.0, "max_tokens": 4000, "n": 1}, "history": null}